WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.110
In language learning, you wanna
do it wrong the first time and you

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.330
want to continue doing it wrong.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:10.170
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here again
to talk about language learning.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:17.400
And in particular, today I'm going to talk
about the need to get it right or, uh,

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:23.190
as, um, one often hears in, feels like
engineering or construction or industrial

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.950
production, get it right the first time.

00:00:27.165 --> 00:00:29.854
Remember, if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe.

00:00:29.865 --> 00:00:33.945
Click on the bell for notifications,
and if you follow me on a podcast

00:00:33.945 --> 00:00:35.085
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:35.085 --> 00:00:35.955
I do appreciate it.

00:00:36.525 --> 00:00:41.445
So I worked for a forest products
company and we were making

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:43.905
lumber and pulp and newsprint.

00:00:44.415 --> 00:00:49.245
And of course, quality control is
very important and as often happens

00:00:49.245 --> 00:00:54.245
in large corporations, we get these
consultants to come in and run programs.

00:00:54.585 --> 00:00:59.355
You know, six sigma and all these
other different, you know, buzzwords

00:00:59.655 --> 00:01:04.125
around the idea of, of improving
quality, like fewer errors.

00:01:04.785 --> 00:01:08.445
And there was one program they had in
one of our mills because the company

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:10.065
was quite sort of decentralized.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:14.715
And this one pulp and paper mill
brought in a consultant who had

00:01:14.935 --> 00:01:17.235
a program called DIRTY FOOT.

00:01:17.835 --> 00:01:20.325
Do it right the first time.

00:01:20.805 --> 00:01:25.335
And all the sort of management people
and, and production people had name

00:01:25.335 --> 00:01:29.085
cards with DIRTY FOOT on the back and
they had a whole bunch of different

00:01:29.085 --> 00:01:32.595
programs around the idea of making
sure that at every step along the

00:01:32.595 --> 00:01:35.565
process, everything was done just right.

00:01:35.625 --> 00:01:37.835
You know, met certain specific standards.

00:01:37.965 --> 00:01:42.815
And the idea was do it right the first
time and then you don't have to redo it.

00:01:43.375 --> 00:01:45.780
Now, how does that apply
to language learning?

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:51.060
Well, the short answer is it doesn't,
doesn't apply in language learning.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:56.970
You wanna do it wrong the first time and
you want to continue doing it wrong, and

00:01:56.975 --> 00:02:01.770
you wanna accept the fact that you're
going to continue doing it wrong if you

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:07.230
are too obsessed with getting it right
the first time or the second time, you

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:13.080
put unnecessary pressure on yourself and
you'll probably hold back and stay with

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:19.260
material that is easy enough so that you
can get it right, which is not a good idea

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:25.080
because you want to expose yourself to
as, as much of the language as possible in

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.540
as many different situations as possible.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.910
And that's going to mean getting it wrong.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:34.020
It's going to mean not understanding,
it's gonna mean answering questions

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.550
wrong with the wrong tense, or the
wrong, uh, preposition, or the wrong

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.440
ending, or any of these things.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:44.520
You're gonna continue getting it wrong,
and you have to be willing to get it

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:50.190
wrong over and over again in order that
you get enough exposure to the language

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:55.590
that eventually you start to get it
right and, and very often I, I think

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:59.430
part of the problem here is the way we
were taught languages at school where

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.490
you don't, obviously you don't wanna
get it wrong on the test, you want to

00:03:02.495 --> 00:03:06.780
get it right on the test, but what if
you aren't going to get it right until

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:10.650
you've actually had enough exposure
or until you've done it wrong enough

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:12.980
times that you start to get it right.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:18.269
So Okay, get it wrong, but don't
penalize people for getting it wrong.

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:21.750
Maybe we congratulate
people for getting it wrong.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:26.670
And encourage them, encourage them
to keep on getting it wrong, uh,

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:29.910
because eventually they're gonna
get it right with enough exposure.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:35.100
So, uh, the idea that you're gonna
master the basics at some early stage,

00:03:35.105 --> 00:03:38.609
like if you're building a house,
well, you've gotta get it right.

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:44.190
Uh, if you don't line things up square
or level, and then the floor isn't

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:49.025
level and you know, the, the stud
goes up and it is isn't at 90 degrees.

00:03:49.025 --> 00:03:55.475
Now the wall isn't square and so many bad
things happen in many industrial processes

00:03:55.985 --> 00:03:57.655
if you don't do it right the first time.

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.900
But that's not the case
in language learning.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:05.520
It's not as if you're you, you're not
building something where if you get

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:09.390
it wrong at an early stage, then you
will build it wrong on top of that.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:14.610
In fact, you will get some things right
and some things wrong, and this will

00:04:14.615 --> 00:04:19.170
continue for quite a while, and at some
point the things you were getting wrong,

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:22.890
you'll start to get them right, but you
can't force yourself to get them right.

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:24.840
At least that's been my experience.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:31.620
I encourage people to accept to own
their mistakes, and that's another reason

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:36.390
why, by the way, why I, why I'm not
a big fan of correcting other people.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:38.190
If they ask for it, fine.

00:04:38.219 --> 00:04:40.980
If they don't, just let them communicate.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:46.409
Let them engage, say things, they
get input back, and gradually they'll

00:04:46.409 --> 00:04:49.230
start to hear a more correct version
of what they were trying to say.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:52.935
Yes, it's possible that some
people will have certain

00:04:52.935 --> 00:04:54.345
errors that they never correct.

00:04:54.345 --> 00:04:57.615
And, and I've seen this, people who
speak English very well, they'll have

00:04:57.615 --> 00:05:02.535
certain patterns from their, uh, native
language that they never ever correct.

00:05:02.585 --> 00:05:07.785
And they continue to use that same pattern
in English, for example, or in some

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:09.225
other language that they're learning.

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:14.775
However, my experience has been, even
if you correct them, they won't change.

00:05:15.165 --> 00:05:21.150
So at some point they have to
naturally feel, develop a new

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.190
habit where this pattern they were.

00:05:24.585 --> 00:05:25.935
, they switch to another pattern.

00:05:26.025 --> 00:05:30.435
But in the meantime, in most cases,
if someone says to me, if a German

00:05:30.495 --> 00:05:35.625
speaking person says, I have lived in
Canada since many years, which is not

00:05:35.625 --> 00:05:39.405
correct in English, it's correct in
German, I understand what he's saying

00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:41.355
or she, so it's not a big problem.

00:05:41.745 --> 00:05:46.185
But if that person has lived in Canada
for a long time and is comfortable

00:05:46.185 --> 00:05:50.200
speaking English, and I then say to
them, well, actually, while we're talking

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:52.840
about something that's of interest to
both of us, and then I jump in there

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:55.930
and say, oh, by the way, you keep on
saying that, but that's not correct.

00:05:56.710 --> 00:05:58.930
If they ask me to correct
their language, fine.

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:00.730
But otherwise I would never do that.

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:05.220
I would just say live with that
mistake, either it'll naturally, you

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:10.350
know, self-correct at some point, or
that person will continue comfortably

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:15.150
communicating in English, even with
the odd trace of say, a German or a

00:06:15.155 --> 00:06:19.470
Swedish or a French or a Japanese or a
Chinese, you know, turn of phrase there,

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:23.790
where based on my experience, even if
I correct them, it won't do much good.

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:30.210
So message is do it right the first
time is very important in industrial

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:34.650
processes, in manufacturing, in
construction and engineering.

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:37.800
But when it comes to language
learning, do it wrong.

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:39.900
Do it wrong, not only the first time.

00:06:39.905 --> 00:06:42.930
Do it wrong many times
and accept your mistakes.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:44.560
There you have it.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:48.330
I look forward to your comments
and I've left a couple of videos

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:51.720
where I talk about the importance
of mistakes in language learning.

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:53.040
Bye for now.

